    _cache[1] || (_cache[1] = createStaticVNode('<h2 id="_3-3-4-hardware-wiring" tabindex="-1">3.3.4 Hardware Wiring <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-4-hardware-wiring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3.4 Hardware Wiring&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Connect the WonderLens vision module cable to the <strong>P4</strong> port of the ESP32 controller.</p><p>Connect the 360° block motor cable to the <strong>S1</strong> port of the ESP32 controller, with the servo&#39;s <strong>orange</strong> wire plugged into the <strong>white</strong> pin of S1.</p><p>Wiring diagram:</p><img loading="lazy" height="1220" width="1100" src="' + _imports_1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:400px;"><h2 id="_3-3-5-programming" tabindex="-1">3.3.5 Programming <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-5-programming" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3.5 Programming&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-program-schematic" tabindex="-1">(1) Program Schematic <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-program-schematic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(1) Program Schematic&quot;">​</a></h3><img loading="lazy" height="400" width="370" src="' + _imports_2 + '" class="common_img" style="width:350px;"><h3 id="_2-adding-extensions" tabindex="-1">(2) Adding Extensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-adding-extensions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(2) Adding Extensions&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Click the <strong>Extensions</strong> button and select the <strong>WonderLens</strong> under the <strong>Sensor</strong> category.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_3 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h3 id="_3-core-blocks" tabindex="-1">(3) Core Blocks <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-core-blocks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(3) Core Blocks&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:center;">Block</th><th style="text-align:center;">Category</th><th style="text-align:left;">Function Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="303" width="408" src="' + _imports_34 + '" class="common_img" style="width:200px;"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="73" src="' + _imports_30 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Compares the left-hand value with a custom value and returns a boolean result indicating greater than.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="41" width="173" src="' + _imports_45 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="73" src="' + _imports_46 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Creates a custom variable to store data.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="115" width="312" src="' + _imports_47 + '" class="common_img" style="width:200px;"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="73" src="' + _imports_46 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Reads data stored in the specified custom variable.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="138" width="522" src="' + _imports_48 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="73" src="' + _imports_46 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Assigns a custom value to the specified variable.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="75" width="553" src="' + _imports_63 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="51" width="75" src="' + _imports_61 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Plays audio according to custom pitch, beat duration, and playback mode.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="108" width="524" src="' + _imports_74 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="51" width="75" src="' + _imports_61 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Controls the servo connected to the specified port to rotate at the set speed.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="412" width="760" src="' + _imports_6 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="55" width="91" src="' + _imports_7 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Switches the AI vision operating mode of the vision module.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="117" width="432" src="' + _imports_8 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="55" width="91" src="' + _imports_7 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Refreshes the camera feed and reads recognition data for the current vision mode.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="90" width="791" src="' + _imports_22 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:center;"><img loading="lazy" height="55" width="91" src="' + _imports_7 + '" class="common_img"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Extracts the number with the highest confidence score from the recognition results.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_4-complete-program" tabindex="-1">(4) Complete Program <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-complete-program" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(4) Complete Program&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><p>Initialization: At startup, initialize the WonderLens vision module at port <strong>P4</strong> and switch the operating mode to number recognition.</p></li><li><p>Main Loop: The program continuously updates and retrieves recognition data from the WonderLens vision module, extracting the number with the highest confidence score and storing it in the variable <code>Result</code>. When <code>Result</code> equals 0, servo <strong>S1</strong> stops rotating. When <code>Result</code> is between 1 and 5, servo <strong>S1</strong> rotates at a speed of 15, sequentially playing tones C4, D4, E4, F4, and G4. Each note is followed by a delay of 0.8 s, and each loop iteration pauses for 0.01 s.</p></li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1227" width="576" src="' + _imports_17 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>The program source files can be downloaded from <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1gpd83ho-Cf-T1LV6VETKvwf1mI7A8Bfg?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Program Files</a>.</strong></p></div><h2 id="_3-3-6-program-upload" tabindex="-1">3.3.6 Program Upload <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-6-program-upload" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3.6 Program Upload&quot;">​</a></h2><img loading="lazy" height="360" width="640" src="' + _imports_79 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h2 id="_3-3-7-expansion-and-challenge" tabindex="-1">3.3.7 Expansion and Challenge <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-7-expansion-and-challenge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3.7 Expansion and Challenge&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Challenge Task 1: RGB Light Synchronized Audio-Visual Performance</strong></p><ol><li><p>Project Goal: When recognizing a number card, illuminate the RGB lights in matching colors while the buzzer plays the melody, creating a unified audio-visual performance. Number 1 illuminates red, number 2 illuminates orange, number 3 illuminates yellow, number 4 illuminates blue, and number 5 illuminates purple.</p></li><li><p>Implementation Strategy: Connect the RGB light module to the controller and insert light color adjustment blocks into each number detection branch. Bind each number to its corresponding light color and melody to trigger audio and visual feedback simultaneously.</p></li><li><p>Key Challenges: Ensuring stable number recognition without skipping digits, eliminating light flickering during color transitions, and synchronizing audio output with lighting effects without lag.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Challenge Task 2: Delayed Stop After Card Removal</strong></p><ol><li><p>Project Goal: When the number card is removed, the music box does not halt immediately. It completes playback for an additional 1 s before muting and gently returning the servo to its center position, simulating the acoustic decay of a mechanical music box.</p></li><li><p>Implementation Strategy: Introduce a timer variable to trigger a playback delay when the card disappears. Sustain the melody and lighting until the delay expires. Mute the buzzer, extinguish the lights, and reset the servo once the timer reaches zero.</p></li><li><p>Key Challenges: Ensuring that the delayed playback routine does not block the camera from detecting new cards, and synchronizing multiple peripherals without system latency.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Challenge Task 3: Creative Applications of Number Recognition</strong></p><ol><li><p>Digital Combination Lock: Retain the core number recognition code, replace the buzzer with an electric lock mechanism, and automatically unlock the mechanism upon recognizing a specific numerical sequence.</p></li><li><p>Quiz Buzzer Scorekeeper: Pair the system with a dot matrix display to increment scores automatically based on detected number cards, updating total scores in real time on the screen.</p></li><li><p>Universal Adaptation Strategy: Keep the core number recognition algorithm unchanged and swap out the output hardware. By implementing new peripheral control blocks under the logic of triggering actions upon detecting specified numbers, diverse smart interactive projects can be developed.</p></li></ol><h2 id="_3-3-8-q-a" tabindex="-1">3.3.8 Q&amp;A <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-8-q-a" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3.8 Q&amp;A&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Question 1: During servo wiring, the orange signal wire must connect to the white pin of port S1. What problems occur if it is wired incorrectly?</strong></p><p><strong>Answer</strong>: The white pin of port <strong>S1</strong> is dedicated to transmitting PWM control signals, which the servo relies on to determine rotation angle and speed. If the orange signal wire connects to the power or ground pins, the controller cannot transmit control instructions. Two primary failures occur: either the servo remains completely motionless so the music box cannot rotate, or the servo vibrates erratically and rotates erratically, potentially overheating and causing hardware damage. Furthermore, all program code designated to control rotation becomes ineffective, preventing synchronized playback and rotation.</p><p><strong>Question 2: The camera recognizes number cards normally and the buzzer sounds, but the servo does not rotate at all. What are the most common causes and troubleshooting steps?</strong></p><p><strong>Answer</strong>:</p><ol><li><p>Wiring Fault: The orange signal wire of the servo is not plugged into the white signal pin of port <strong>S1</strong>. Troubleshooting: Disconnect the power, then verify the three-pin sequence against the wiring diagram.</p></li><li><p>Program Fault: The servo rotation code is placed outside the condition branch for detecting number cards. Troubleshooting: Open the program and verify that the servo rotation and delay blocks reside inside the conditional block that executes when number recognition succeeds.</p></li><li><p>Hardware Damage: The servo itself is defective or port <strong>S1</strong> of the ESP32 controller is damaged. Troubleshooting: Connect a known working servo to port <strong>S1</strong> for testing. If it still fails to move, connect the servo to an alternative servo port on the controller to isolate the issue.</p></li></ol><h2 id="_3-3-9-technical-support-and-product-discussion" tabindex="-1">3.3.9 Technical Support and Product Discussion <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-9-technical-support-and-product-discussion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3.9 Technical Support and Product Discussion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Share project ideas and connect with other makers on the <a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hiwonder community</a>:</p><a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img loading="lazy" height="640" width="1136" src="' + _imports_12 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"></a>', 35))
